WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.390
At a time when it seems like every movie in
history is being considered for a remake or

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:08.480
a reboot, the horror genre has found itself
particularly ripe for pillaging.

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:11.440
Sometimes, it takes more than just one attempt
to get it right.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:15.880
For proof, just queue up these horror remakes
that actually turned out better than the original

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:16.380
films.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:17.520
The Evil Dead

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:22.060
The horror genre wouldn't be the same without
Sam Raimi's 1981 classic The Evil Dead.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.660
Made on a shoestring budget with no-name actors,
the film used guerrilla special effects and

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:29.040
wildly inventive camerawork to sell its story.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.120
Many fans who were still stung by the failed
remakes of Nightmare on Elm Street and Friday

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.060
the 13th were understandably skeptical when
the 2013 Evil Dead remake was announced.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.020
What they didn't know was that director Fede
Alvarez's new version, which Raimi produced,

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.460
would turn out to be one of the best horror
films of the decade.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:50.360
"I'm really excited to see something that
I care about really deeply being reborn by

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:51.440
a great artist."

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:57.679
The 2013 film is tense, well-acted, and efficiently
paced, with the kind of jaw-dropping practical

00:00:57.679 --> 00:01:02.219
effects Hollywood hadn't seen in years — a
ridiculously underrated film, and an improvement

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.680
on the beloved but understandably flawed original.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:12.240
"I will rip your soul out, daddy...I'll rip
your soul out!"

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.400
The Hills Have Eyes

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.280
French filmmaker Alexandre Aja earned his
ticket to Hollywood with his stylishly effective

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.020
2003 film High Tension.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:26.180
In turn, he was handed the remake of Wes Craven's
1977 cult classic The Hills Have Eyes.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:30.800
Released in 2006, the film garnered middling
reviews, despite being as stylish and effective

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.560
as High Tension, and ratcheting the gore way
up.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.420
Not that the remake is a great film, but it's
far more effective than the original — which

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.760
may be the most overrated horror film ever.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.920
Craven's Hills was released before the advent
of home video, when a film's legend often

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:48.190
grew by word of mouth, which is likely why
the original Hills is remembered as anything

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:49.320
but laughably inept.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:53.540
Luckily, seven years later, Craven crafted
a true classic, Nightmare on Elm Street.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.060
[Screaming]

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:58.080
The Crazies

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:03.140
George A. Romero is a revered filmmaker for
one simple reason: he created zombies as we

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:07.020
know them, introducing the concept in his
low-budget masterpiece Night of the Living

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:07.520
Dead.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:12.440
But with 1973's The Crazies, he branched out
with a story about inhabitants of a small

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:15.960
town turning into maniacs when exposed to
an experimental virus.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:20.220
Unfortunately, the only place the film ever
gained much of an audience was the video store.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.900
It was a box office failure — because it
wasn't very good.

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:27.880
The 2010 remake was the sophomore effort of
Sahara filmmaker Breck Eisner.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.319
Understandably, nobody was expecting much
from the retelling of a of a little-loved,

00:02:31.319 --> 00:02:35.730
decades-old film — but what they got was
a shockingly well-made, superbly photographed,

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:39.960
and at times unbearably tense film that bears
little resemblance to its schlocky, B-movie

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:40.460
namesake.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:45.240
Remaking Romero is hardly ever a great idea,
but Eisner's The Crazies is simply excellent

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:45.920
in its own right.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:47.100
"Is he dead?"

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:49.040
[Screaming]

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:50.660
Funny Games

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.950
With his 1997 German-language horror film
Funny Games, Michael Haneke wanted to challenge

00:02:55.950 --> 00:03:00.180
the audience, implicating them in onscreen
carnage in a way that no film had before.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:05.060
It's an excellent and disturbing piece, and
many of its fans saw the 2007 remake — also

00:03:05.069 --> 00:03:09.560
directed by Haneke, a nearly shot-for-shot
recreation of the original — as completely

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:10.240
unnecessary.

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:12.980
But by a slight margin, it's the superior
film.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:17.240
One could go so far as to say that the 2007
version is the one Haneke intended to make

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:21.280
in the first place, since his goal was to
target an American audience — something

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:22.540
the original failed to do.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:26.860
It also benefits from a pair of truly talented
leads in Naomi Watts and Tim Roth.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:30.710
The debate around this oddly unique pair of
films will likely never be settled, and they're

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:34.800
both fantastic — but the 2007 version is
ever-so-slightly better.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:36.240
Silent House

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:43.200
The 2010 Uruguayan film La Casa Muda, shot
entirely on a $6,000 budget, is quite an achievement.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:48.080
It's edited to appear as if it's one continuous,
88-minute shot, following a young woman who's

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.400
shown up to help renovate a creepy old house.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:54.190
The single-take gimmick is well done, but
the film suffers from the rough edges you'd

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:57.000
expect from a shoestring production with an
unknown cast.

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:01.920
By contrast, the 2011 remake Silent House
benefits from putting its star, Elizabeth

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.720
Olsen, front and center in every shot.

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:08.090
Olsen completely sells the mounting terror
as things go from weird in the first act,

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:11.500
to terrifying in the second act, to sanity-shattering
in the third.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:16.020
Silent House is a heavy-duty upgrade to its
ultra-low budget predecessor — not to mention

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:17.280
criminally underrated.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:19.060
We Are What We Are

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:25.140
The 2010 Mexican film Somos Lo Que Hay was
a confounding piece of work: a dark, brooding,

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.700
well-made psychodrama about a family of cannibals.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:32.040
It was well-received, but suffered from a
tone that some called "relentlessly humorless,"

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.600
and fielded a cast that at times was less
than engaging.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:39.540
The English-language 2013 version borrows
the basic premise of the original, but not

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:40.160
much else.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:44.760
It trades the 2010 film's urban setting for
the backwoods of the deep South, injecting

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:48.520
an American Gothic feel that's much more appropriate
for the lurid subject matter.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:52.820
Director Jim Mickle uses the material to raise
questions about the dangers of tradition and

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:56.990
religion that were completely absent from
the original, and he trades that film's ending

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:01.100
for one of the most bizarre, haunting conclusions
of any horror film this decade.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:05.260
We Are What We Are might not exactly scare
your pants off, but it will stick with you

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:06.380
long after viewing.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:08.920
"Tell daddy supper's ready."

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:10.880
Thanks for watching!

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.260
